Comment by al...@bedatadriven.com:

Score: Positive

General Comment:
Lot's of good work here!

Line-by-line comments:

File: /trunk/core/src/main/java/r/base/random/Beta.java (r317)
===============================================================================

Line 27:   /* FIXME:  Keep Globals (properly) for threading */
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
It might be worth organizing the random number generators into a set of
class implementing some sort of Distribution interface similar to the
commons math API:

interface Distribution {
    double sample();
}

public class BetaDistribution implements Distribution  {
    private final double beta, gamma, delta, k1, k2;

    public BetaDistribution(double aa, double bb) {
      // save dist parameters
      // do expensive init here
    }

    public double sample() {
     // impl
    }
}

File: /trunk/core/src/main/java/r/base/random/RNG.java (r317)
===============================================================================

Line 87:   public static DoubleVector runif(int n, double a, double b) {
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The random generation functions allow recycling of the distribution
parameters :-(

So try :

mean(rnorm(10000, mean=c(0,50)))

Unfortunately this recycling doesn't work in the standard way because the
total number of cycles is dictated by the n argument rather than by simply
the lengths of the recycled vectors.

We could handle this at the code generation level (e.g. a @Cycles
annotation that you could annotate n with), but for some distributions
where initialization is expensive, it may make more sense to handle it
explicitly.

For more information:
http://code.google.com/p/renjin/source/detail?r=317

